From: nuwa smp bring-up
Subject: [PATCH] ArmCpu: admit at most one secondary (the ADT's 0x100 core)

sheng-smp is the single opt-in for secondary CPU start-up.  Until now a
nonzero value let IOCPU probe every cpu node the topology knows about
(MAX_CPUS 4 keeps cpu0..cpu3, so three secondaries would be registered and
started).  The bring-up test needs exactly one secondary, the first one the
device tree numbers 0x100, so that a failure has one cause and the boot core
keeps a single peer.

Every other cpu node stays unprobed, i.e. it remains in the device tree's
"waiting" state and no PSCI CPU_ON is issued for it.  With sheng-smp absent
the branch is unchanged: the function returns nullptr exactly as before and
the boot-only path is untouched.

--- a/drivers/ArmCpu/ArmCpu.cpp
+++ b/drivers/ArmCpu/ArmCpu.cpp
@@ -223,6 +223,12 @@
     if (smp == 0 || ml_get_cpu_number (id) < 0 || ml_get_cpu_number (id) >= ARMCPU_SMP_SLOTS) {
       return nullptr; // Secondary startup is opt-in (sheng-smp=1).
     }
+    // One secondary only: the ADT numbers the SM8550 cores in Affinity1, so the first
+    // secondary reports reg 0x100. Every other node stays unprobed (waiting) and no
+    // CPU_ON is issued for it.
+    if (id != 0x100) {
+      return nullptr;
+    }
   }
   return IOCPU::probe (provider, score);
 }
